export const GUIDE_TITLE = "Writing in Shrubbery";
export const LEGACY_EXAMPLE_HASH = 4151971916;
const BASE_GUIDE = "{{page background=canopy accent=olive}}\n# WRITING IN SHRUBBERY\n### {{accent An editable guide to your Wildsea workshop}}\n\nThis document is the guide and the demonstration. Everything on this page comes from the Markdown on the left. Change a heading, edit a sentence, or copy a block and watch the preview update. **Your edits save automatically in this browser.**\n\n{{columns,two-one\n## Start with the basics\nUse **My documents → New voyage** for a blank document, or experiment with this guide. **Open guide** creates a fresh guide without replacing your work. The title at the top of the app names your saved file; the headings in the editor belong to the printed page.\n\n{{box\n### Try your first edit\nReplace the main heading in the editor. Then choose **Blocks → Leaf bullet list** to add a list at your cursor. Undo with Ctrl/Cmd+Z if you want to try again.\n\nThis rounded outline is a box. Its source starts with `{{box` and ends with `}}` on its own line.\n}}\n\n## Format your writing\n{{columns,two\n### Headings and emphasis\nStart a line with `#` for a main title, `##` for a section, or `###` for a smaller heading. Put a space after the hashes.\n\nWrap words in `**` for **bold**, or `*` for *italics*. Separate paragraphs with a blank line. Text wraps to fit its column automatically.\n\n### Links\nWrite `[label](https://example.com)` for a link. An image uses `![description](https://example.com/image.png)`. Images fit the available width.\n\n\\column\n\n### Lists\nAn ordinary Markdown list uses the supplied Wildsea bullet automatically:\n\n- Start each item with a dash and a space.\n- Keep each item on its own source line.\n- Let long items wrap naturally in the preview.\n\nFor a numbered list, start lines with `1.`, `2.`, and so on.\n\n1. Write the content.\n2. Choose the layout.\n3. Check the finished page.\n}}\n\n\\column\n\n{{box\n## Find your tools\n- **Tables** offers striped, plain, ruled and roll tables.\n- **Columns** offers equal and wide/narrow layouts.\n- **Blocks** adds boxes, lists and tracks.\n- **Page templates** adds a reach, character, hazard, scenario or quickplay page.\n- **Page style** selects document defaults.\n- **Spreads** shows pages side by side.\n- **Fit / zoom** changes preview size only.\n- **?** opens a compact syntax reference.\n}}\n\n## About these pages\nPage 2 demonstrates boxes, striped fields, and tables. Page 3 explains columns, backgrounds, saving, and printing.\n\nThe guide uses explicit page colours. Change the `{{page ...}}` line at the top of a page to change that page's appearance.\n\n{{flavor\nThis is a flavour-text block. Use it for a short quotation or a prominent aside.\n\nIts source begins with {{muted flavor}}.\n}}\n}}\n\n\\page\n\n{{page background=reach accent=mustard}}\n{{box,black\n## BOXES, FIELDS & TABLES {{accent (Independent colour controls)}}\nThis wide box has a black border and a mustard accent. Its source opens with `{{box,black`. The border is explicitly black; the subtitle and stripes inherit mustard from this page. Use `accent=red` on a block only when you want to override the page colour. The interior stays white.\n\nA subtitle is an inline span: `{{accent (Independent colour controls)}}`. Put the main heading and subtitle on the same line, as demonstrated above. Close the surrounding box with `}}` on a line by itself.\n}}\n\n{{columns,three\n{{box,black\n### Striped fields {{accent (One line per entry)}}\nUse `{{fields` for labelled facts or results. Each non-empty source line becomes one row; the first, third, and subsequent odd rows are highlighted.\n\n{{fields\n**First entry:** Start a row with a bold label, followed by its description.\n**Second entry:** This row has no highlight.\n**Third entry:** A long entry can wrap across several printed lines. Its background covers the whole entry, not just its first line.\n**Fourth entry:** Put the next entry on a new source line, then close the fields block with }} on a separate line.\n}}\n\nThe bold labels use ordinary Markdown. You can choose any labels; they are not fixed form fields.\n}}\n\n\\column\n\n{{box,black\n### Markdown tables {{accent (Rows and columns)}}\nUse a standard pipe table for information that needs aligned columns. Choose **Tables → Striped table** to get the syntax.\n\n| Element | What it does |\n|:---|:---|\n| Header row | Names each column. |\n| Separator row | Uses dashes between pipes. |\n| Body rows | Alternate pale and white. |\n| Bold text | Works inside cells. |\n\nThe header takes the accent colour. The body uses its pale companion tint. A table can appear inside a box or directly on the page.\n\nFor headerless entries such as Essentials and Attitude, use a fields block instead.\n}}\n\n\\column\n\n{{box,black\n### Multiple sections {{accent (One enclosure)}}\nA single box can hold more than one heading, paragraph, or fields block. This column demonstrates two sections inside the same outline.\n\n{{fields\n**Heading:** Use a new Markdown heading within the box.\n**Spacing:** Leave a blank line between paragraphs and blocks.\n}}\n\n### Nesting blocks {{accent (Close each one)}}\nClose the inner fields block before closing the outer box. Each opening block needs its own closing line.\n\n{{fields\n**Inner block:** Close the fields first.\n**Outer block:** Close the box next.\n}}\n\nAn unfinished block still previews, but a warning tells you to add the missing closing marker.\n}}\n}}\n\n\\page\n\n{{page background=canopy accent=blue}}\n# LAYOUT, SAVING & PRINTING\nA page is **10 × 8.5 inches**. Page and column breaks are deliberate: the editor will warn when content runs beyond a page, rather than silently move it to another one. Add `\\page` on its own line to begin the next page.\n\n{{columns,two-one\n## Choose a column layout\nThis page uses `{{columns,two-one`: a wide left column and a narrow right column. A `\\column` line separates them. Text above the columns block spans the page; text after its closing marker does too.\n\n{{columns,two\n### Equal columns\nUse `{{columns,two` for two equal columns or `{{columns,three` for three. Place `\\column` between each column, then end the whole layout with `}}`.\n\nThese two sections are a nested two-column block inside the wide part of the outer layout. This is how you can reproduce the arrangement on Tigers page 18.\n\n\\column\n\n### Wide and narrow\nUse `two-one` for wide left / narrow right, or `one-two` for the reverse. Choose **Columns → Wide left, narrow right** to start.\n\nTo make a Tigers page 9 arrangement, put a full-width box before a three-column block. Place one box in each column, as on the previous guide page.\n}}\n\n## Backgrounds and colours\n**Black is the default**, so new documents are monochrome. **Page style** sets the document's background, accent and first page number. A page-specific line such as `{{page background=reach accent=mustard}}` overrides the background and accent for that page only. Remove that line to inherit the document defaults. Boxes, subtitles, tables, fields and tracks inherit the page accent; you only need to name a colour for an override.\n\n{{fields\n**Backgrounds:** canopy, reach, plain. The first two use the supplied InDesign artwork.\n**Greens:** willow, olive, malachite.\n**Other accents:** blue, mustard, raspberry, red, black.\n**Box colours:** The outline and accent can be selected independently.\n}}\n\n## Keep a copy of your work\n**Browser drafts** save automatically on this device. Use **My documents** to switch drafts, create a new one, or reopen this guide. Download a copy before clearing browser data or changing browsers.\n\n**Download Markdown** includes the source and document settings. **Import Markdown** opens a file as a new draft. It does not replace the document you were editing.\n\n**Google Drive** provides explicit Save, Save new copy, and Open actions after you connect. It requires a dedicated Wildsea OAuth client; the Drive dialog includes setup instructions. Browser autosave does not automatically save to Drive.\n\n\\column\n\n{{box\n## Print a clean PDF\n1. Check that there are no overflow or unfinished-block warnings.\n2. Choose **Print / PDF**.\n3. Select your browser's PDF destination.\n4. Keep the scale at **100%**.\n5. Turn **background graphics on**.\n6. Turn browser **headers and footers off**.\n\nMost pages print at 10 × 8.5 inches; Quickplay uses 11 × 8.5 inches. Preview zoom and spread mode do not change the PDF. Printing always produces individual pages.\n}}\n\n## If a page overflows\nShorten the text, move a section onto a new page, or change the column arrangement. Long words and large images can also cause overflow.\n\n## Tracks and trackbreaks\nWrite `{{track 3|2|5}}` for ten circles with breaks after circles 3 and 5:\n\n{{track 3|2|5}}\n\nUse `{{track 6}}` for an unbroken track. **Blocks → Track with breaks** adds an editable example. Each number sets a group length.\n\n**Ctrl/Cmd+S** saves the browser draft. **Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+S** saves to Drive when connected.\n}}\n";

